<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The hardened runtime is required for notarization, and it forbids
       writable-executable memory by default. WKWebView's JavaScript engine
       needs exactly that to run at all: without this the window comes up
       and every page inside it is dead. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>            <true/>

  <!-- Deliberately absent, and each for a reason:

       * App Sandbox — Petrel is distributed outside the App Store and keeps
         its mail in Application Support, reads attachments the user picks,
         and writes exports where the user says. Sandboxing that would mean
         security-scoped bookmarks for paths the user already chose, which
         buys nothing outside the store's own rules.
       * allow-unsigned-executable-memory / disable-library-validation —
         the blunter escapes. WKWebView needs the JIT entitlement above and
         nothing more; asking for the broader ones would widen the app's
         attack surface to save reading this comment.
       * Keychain access groups — the keychain works outside the sandbox
         without an entitlement; an access group would only matter if
         Petrel shared secrets with a sibling app, and it has none. -->
</dict>
</plist>
